# This repository is consumed on Linux - by Docker build containers and by
# shell scripts that parse checked-in data files - but it is routinely cloned
# on Windows, where git's default core.autocrlf=true rewrites every text file
# on checkout. That breaks the repository's central claim (rebuild from a
# clean checkout) and it breaks it SILENTLY-ISH:
#
#   * build/build-kernel.sh gets a "#!/bin/bash\r" shebang, and the kernel
#     reports "no such file or directory" for a file that plainly exists.
#     Measured here: a fresh `git clone` on Windows, then
#     `docker start -a` => `exec ./build/build-kernel.sh: no such file or
#     directory`.
#   * kernel/patches/*.patch gain CRLF, so `git apply` either refuses them or
#     applies content that differs byte-for-byte from what CI applied - which
#     would change the artifact hash without changing the pin.
#   * kernel/source.lock is parsed with `cut -d= -f2-`, so every pinned value
#     would carry a trailing CR (a commit sha that never matches, a
#     BOOT_PARTITION_BYTES that fails integer comparison).
#
# a50-droidian already carries this file for the same reason. Force LF.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.patch text eol=lf
*.diff text eol=lf
*.config text eol=lf
*.lock text eol=lf
*.sha256 text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

*.img binary
*.gz binary
*.xz binary
*.zip binary
